The problem asks for the minimum number of operations to make a string of 'B's (black) and 'W's (white) entirely 'W's. An operation consists of choosing a substring of length $k$ and turning all characters in it to 'W'.

Let's analyze the operation. We want to cover all 'B's with intervals of length $k$. If we have a 'B' at index $i$, we must choose an interval $[j, j+k-1]$ such that $j \le i < j+k$. This effectively means we need to select a set of intervals of length $k$ such that every index $i$ where $s[i] == 'B'$ is covered by at least one interval. We want to minimize the number of intervals.

This looks like a greedy problem. We scan the string from left to right. When we encounter the first 'B' that hasn't been covered yet, we must cover it. To maximize the utility of this operation for future 'B's, we should place the interval as far to the right as possible while still covering the current 'B'.

Let's formalize this. Suppose we are at index $i$ and $s[i] == 'B'$. This 'B' needs to be covered. The interval covering it must start at some index $start$ such that $start \le i$ and $start + k - 1 \ge i$. To help cover subsequent 'B's (which are at indices $> i$), we want the interval to extend as far right as possible. The rightmost end of the interval is fixed at $i + k - 1$ if we start at $i$. Wait, the interval has length $k$. If we want to cover index $i$, the interval could be $[i, i+k-1]$, $[i-1, i+k-2]$, ..., $[i-k+1, i]$. The one that extends furthest to the right is $[i, i+k-1]$.
So, the greedy strategy is:
1. Iterate through the string from left to right.
2. Keep track of the index of the last operation performed. Let's say the last operation covered the range $[last\_start, last\_start + k - 1]$.
3. If we encounter a 'B' at index $i$ that is not covered by the previous operation (i.e., $i > last\_start + k - 1$), we must perform a new operation.
4. To cover this 'B' at $i$ and extend as far right as possible, we should start the new operation at index $i$. This operation will cover the range $[i, i+k-1]$.
5. Increment the operation count and update $last\_start = i$.

Let's trace this logic with an example.
